WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.129
Are Tuvalu's islands shrinking?

00:00:02.129 --> 00:00:07.007
In 2018 Paul Kench and colleagues measured every one of the country's one hundred and

00:00:07.007 --> 00:00:08.384
one islands to find out.

00:00:09.484 --> 00:00:14.411
They compared aerial photographs from 1971 with satellite images from 2014.

00:00:14.411 --> 00:00:19.074
Over those four decades local sea level rose about fifteen centimetres,

00:00:19.074 --> 00:00:20.717
twice the global average.

00:00:20.717 --> 00:00:25.380
Yet Tuvalu's total land area grew by seventy-three and a half hectares,

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:26.760
nearly three percent.

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:31.374
Here is the change for each atoll, from the paper's Table 1.

00:00:31.374 --> 00:00:33.542
Eight of the nine atolls gained land.

00:00:33.542 --> 00:00:35.592
Funafuti gained about four percent.

00:00:36.692 --> 00:00:38.056
Now be a careful reader.

00:00:38.056 --> 00:00:42.432
Add up the hectare changes in Table 1 and you get about sixty-three hectares,

00:00:42.432 --> 00:00:44.080
not seventy-three and a half.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:46.581
And one percentage in the table looks wrong.

00:00:46.581 --> 00:00:49.082
Small inconsistencies like these are common.

00:00:49.082 --> 00:00:52.492
Your task is to work out whether they change the conclusion.

00:00:52.492 --> 00:00:55.674
Here they do not: most islands grew under every reading.

00:00:56.774 --> 00:00:59.970
The authors conclude that islands are dynamic features,

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:02.178
not static landforms waiting to drown.

00:01:02.178 --> 00:01:05.199
But they are careful: under high-emission scenarios,

00:01:05.199 --> 00:01:10.428
the sea will rise much faster than in their study period, and the past may not be a guide.
